  "prediction": "**Pacific Crest Trail Overcrowding and Hiking Trends**\n\nThe Pacific Crest Trail (PCT) has seen a significant increase in hikers in recent years, particularly since the release of Cheryl Strayed's memoir \"Wild\" and the subsequent movie adaptation. According to the Pacific Crest Trail Association (PCTA), more than 4,000 long-distance hiking permits have been issued this year, with over 3,000 hikers attempting the Northbound thru-hike. This surge in popularity has raised concerns about overcrowding and the impact on the trail and its environment.\n\nThe PCTA has implemented a quota system, limiting the number of hikers to 50 per day, to mitigate wear and tear on the trail and prevent environmental damage. However, this system has been criticized for being too restrictive, with some hikers feeling that it takes away from the spirit of the trail.\n\nDespite the challenges, many hikers continue to be drawn to the PCT, with some reporting that the trail has become more crowded and commercialized. The PCTA has launched a campaign, #responsiblywild, to promote safety and responsible hiking practices, including protecting water sources, burying human waste, and building safe campfires.\n\nThe increase in hikers has also led to a rise in rescue operations, with over 20 rescues reported in the first 50 miles of the trail this year. The PCTA estimates that the maximum number of allowable hikers of 50 per day is being reached during the high season.\n\nWhile some hikers are concerned about the impact of overcrowding, others see it as an opportunity to connect with like-minded individuals and experience the beauty of the trail. The PCT remains a popular destination for hikers, with many reporting that the experience has been life-changing.\n\n**Statistics:**\n\n* Over 4,000 long-distance hiking permits issued this year\n* Over 3,000 hikers attempting the Northbound thru-hike\n* 50 hikers per day quota system implemented by the PCTA\n* Over 20 rescues reported in the first 50 miles of the trail this year\n* Maximum number of allowable hikers of 50 per day reached during high season\n\n**Trends:**\n\n* Increase in female hikers, with anecdotal evidence suggesting that more women are hitting the trail\n* Rise in section-hikers, who take on shorter sections of the trail\n* Growing popularity of the PCT, with many hikers reporting that the experience has been life-changing\n\n**Concerns",
